+  <article class="journal" id="roasters-journal" aria-labelledby="journal-title">
+    <h2 id="journal-title">Roaster's Journal</h2>
+    <p class="journal-byline">Notes from the roastery on how our beans find their way to your cup</p>
+
+    <div class="summary-panel">
+      <button type="button" class="btn" id="summarize-btn" disabled>Summarize</button>
+      <p class="summary-status" id="summary-status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
+      <ul class="summary-list" id="summary-list" aria-live="polite"></ul>
+    </div>
+
+    <div class="journal-body" id="journal-body">
+      <p>Every bag of coffee we roast starts with a relationship, not a transaction. Long before a single bean crosses our threshold, our green coffee team has spent months, and sometimes years, getting to know the farmers, cooperatives, and washing stations that grow it. We travel to origin at least twice a year, timing our visits to the harvest so we can walk the rows, taste cherries straight off the branch, and see firsthand how each farm processes its crop.</p>
+
+      <h3>Finding the right partners</h3>
+      <p>We currently work with fourteen producing partners across Ethiopia, Colombia, Guatemala, Kenya, and Sumatra. Most of these relationships began with a single sample sent to us by an importer we trust, but they only became partnerships after we visited in person. We look for growers who share our obsession with quality and who treat their land and workers with care. That means shade-grown trees wherever the climate allows, responsible water use at the wet mill, and fair wages for the pickers who do the hardest work of the season.</p>
+      <p>We pay, on average, 2.4 times the Fairtrade minimum price for our green coffee, and we publish what we pay each farm on the back of every bag. We believe transparency keeps us honest, and it gives our customers a clear picture of where their money goes. When a farm reaches a quality milestone, we share a premium on top of the agreed price so that improvements in the field are rewarded directly.</p>
+
+      <h3>Cupping and selection</h3>
+      <p>Once samples arrive at the roastery, they go through a blind cupping process. Three members of our team taste each lot independently, scoring it for aroma, acidity, body, sweetness, and finish. Only coffees that score 85 points or higher on the Specialty Coffee Association scale make it into our lineup. We reject far more lots than we accept, but we always send detailed feedback to the producer so that next year's harvest has a better chance.</p>
+      <p>Moisture content and density matter just as much as flavor. Green coffee that is too wet can develop mold in transit, while beans that are too dry lose their vibrancy quickly. We test every lot for moisture and water activity before committing to a purchase, and we store our green coffee in climate-controlled, GrainPro-lined bags to keep it fresh for as long as possible.</p>
+
+      <h3>From the farm to our roaster</h3>
+      <p>Most of our coffee travels by sea in shipping containers, which is far more carbon-efficient than air freight. We consolidate shipments with other small roasters whenever we can to reduce empty space and cost. When the beans arrive, we roast in small batches on a 15-kilo drum roaster, developing a unique profile for each origin. A washed Ethiopian might get a quick, light roast to preserve its floral notes, while a Sumatran wet-hulled lot benefits from a slower, deeper development that brings out its earthy, chocolatey character.</p>
+      <p>We roast five days a week and ship within 48 hours, so the coffee in your cup is never more than a few days off the roaster. It's a lot of work, but it's the only way we know to honor the people who grew it.</p>
+    </div>
